Size: the ML size inliner for Linux and macOS too.

Chromium inlines with a model trained for size when it builds 32-bit
Android (use_ml_inliner), and asserts that nothing else asks for it,
because it was not known whether the model helps other targets. On x86-64
it does: twelve large engine sources (Blink, V8, content, net, Skia, cc)
compiled 21.6% smaller with it at -Os. This lets Linux builds use it, and
macOS builds, which zebkit makes on Linux too, from a Linux host as
before. On in profiles/slim.gn.

Chromium's -Os configuration then passes the model to the compiler, which
does all of the engine's inlining: the engine is linked without LTO
optimization. Code built at another level does not get it: V8 (-O3), the
-Oz targets of patches/1007-min-size-for-chosen-targets.patch, and the hot
paths that file lets a profile name.

--- a/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
+++ b/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
@@ -1210,9 +1210,11 @@
   #    non-Android targets.
   # MLGO is only officially supported on linux.
   if (use_ml_inliner && is_a_target_toolchain) {
+    # zebkit: also for Linux and macOS. The size model makes x86-64 code
+    # smaller too.
     assert(
-        is_android && host_os == "linux",
-        "MLGO is currently only supported for targeting Android on a linux host")
+        (is_android || is_linux || is_mac) && host_os == "linux",
+        "MLGO is currently only supported for targeting Android, Linux or macOS on a linux host")
     if (use_thin_lto) {
       ldflags += [ "-Wl,-mllvm,-enable-ml-inliner=release" ]
       if (is_high_end_android) {
